ia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">An int=
ention to destroy=3F</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,se=
rif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The case against Myanmar revolved aro=
und the question of intent. Did Myanmar intend to destroy the Rohingya, in =
whole or in part, as an ethnic or religious group=3F</span></p><p style=3D"=
font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Myanm=
ar, which has disputed the charges, argued that its actions constituted a c=
ounterterrorism operation =E2=80=94 one aimed at Rohingya militants who had=
 attacked its security posts.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px g=
eorgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The court, looking at a reco=
rd that included attacks against children and efforts by security forces to=
 systematically shoot at fleeing Rohingya, disagreed.</span></p><p style=3D=
"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>=E2=
=80=9CThe only reasonable inference that can explain Myanmar=E2=80=99s acti=
ons is an intention to destroy a substantial part of the group,=E2=80=9D Ju=
dge Yuji Iwasawa, the president of the I.C.J., read from the ruling.</span>=
</p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 1=
5px"><span>It will likely be years before the court hands down a decision o=
n whether Israel=E2=80=99s actions in Gaza following the Hamas-led Oct. 7, =
2023, attacks constituted a genocide, in a case brought by South Africa in =
2023. But there are some parallels between the two cases, </span><a href=3D=
"https://nl.nytimes.com/f/newsletter/AtE9N2v4SrEfsPeO9NR9Bw~~/AAAAARA~/Mcax=
JKm5WoAlV9g7ymTy8AUOCqPTEoiEiTWAlrV6ZgCDEXlfLKB94vo9iR3QHNjEwlgUDzBCnSXqH1o=
